    <h1>Chihuahuan Raven Diet: What This Desert Raven Eats</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Chihuahuan Raven is a highly opportunistic omnivore adapted to the desert grasslands and scrub of the southwestern USA and northern Mexico. Its diet includes carrion, insects and other invertebrates, small vertebrates, plant material (especially cultivated grain and corn), eggs, and human food waste. Foraging habits are flexible and vary seasonally with food availability.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Primary Food Items</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Studies of Chihuahuan Raven diet (primarily from stomach contents and field observation in Texas, New Mexico, and adjacent Mexico) identify the following major food categories:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Carrion:</strong> carcasses of cattle, sheep, and wild animals (pronghorn, jackrabbits, roadkill). Access to livestock ranching operations, with their associated carcasses, is an important food source in much of this species' range.</li>
      <li><strong>Insects and invertebrates:</strong> beetles, grasshoppers, caterpillars, and other invertebrates form a significant dietary component, especially in summer when insect abundance is high. Chihuahuan Ravens forage actively on the ground for invertebrates in a way that contrasts with the larger Common Raven's greater reliance on vertebrate prey and carrion.</li>
      <li><strong>Plant material:</strong> corn (maize), grain sorghum, sunflower seeds, cactus fruits, and other plant foods. The species' use of cultivated crops is well documented and is highest in autumn when harvests provide concentrated food.</li>
      <li><strong>Small vertebrates:</strong> lizards, mice, and small birds — taken opportunistically rather than as a staple.</li>
      <li><strong>Eggs:</strong> eggs of ground-nesting birds including quail and other grassland species. Chihuahuan Ravens are documented nest predators of some ground-nesting birds.</li>
      <li><strong>Human food waste:</strong> garbage, roadside food scraps, and food near human habitation — a consistent dietary supplement in the human-modified landscapes this species inhabits.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Foraging Behaviour</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Chihuahuan Ravens forage in several ways depending on food type:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ground foraging:</strong> walking and probing the ground for invertebrates and plant material. The species covers considerable distance on foot in short grass and bare soil habitats.</li>
      <li><strong>Communal carcass feeding:</strong> like Common Ravens, Chihuahuan Ravens aggregate at large food sources. Flocks at carcasses or refuse sites can number dozens to hundreds of birds — this species is notably more colonial in its foraging and roosting than the Common Raven.</li>
      <li><strong>Crop raiding:</strong> Chihuahuan Ravens visit grain fields in flocks, particularly in autumn. This colonial crop-raiding behaviour has historically brought them into conflict with farmers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Diet Compared to Common Raven</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Where Chihuahuan and Common Ravens overlap (much of New Mexico and southern Colorado), their diets are broadly similar but with differences reflecting their different typical habitats. The Common Raven, associated with mountains, forests, and coasts, relies more heavily on large carcasses; the Chihuahuan Raven, a grassland-desert bird, relies more on invertebrates and plant material. The Chihuahuan Raven's distinctive white feather bases (<em>cryptoleucus</em>) are not visible in normal plumage — see <a href="/compare/chihuahuan-raven-vs-common-raven/">Chihuahuan vs Common Raven comparison</a>.</p>
  </section>

